※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=42 diag=none

※ An `else` arm is what runs where the loop ran out rather than being left by a
※ `break`.  It is what that way through the loop comes to, so a loop with one
※ comes to a plain value rather than a result: both ways give something, and
※ there is nothing left for a failure to mean.
※
※ Which is the whole of the rule: a loop comes to what every way out of it
※ gives, and where one of the ways gives nothing, that is what the result says.

fn root_of(square: u8) → u8:
    foreach §looking i := 0u8…10u8:
        if i × i = square:
            break §looking i
    else:
        99u8

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    let n: mut u8 = 0u8
    ※ a `while` takes one too, and its else arm runs when the condition stops
    let counted: u8 = while §counting n < 10u8:
        n ← n + 1u8
        if n = 6u8:
            break §counting n × 7u8
    else:
        1u8
    ※ 42 where the break was taken; the arm gives 1 where it was not
    let ran_out: u8 = while §quiet false:
        break §quiet 0u8
    else:
        0u8
    counted - root_of(49u8) + ran_out + 7u8
